From: Elie Bursztein <github@elie.net>
Subject: [PATCH] virtiofs: allow idmapped mounts without FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP

FUSE marks every superblock SB_I_NOIDMAP and clears it only when the
daemon negotiates FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP on a default_permissions mount. The
opt-in exists for daemons that check permissions against the uid in the
request header, which an idmapped mount rewrites.

virtio-fs always mounts with default_permissions, so the guest kernel
makes every permission decision itself, through the mount's idmap.
Apple's Virtualization.framework daemon never negotiates
FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP and ignores ownership altogether: every entry is 0:0 and
chown is accepted without effect. mount_setattr(MOUNT_ATTR_IDMAP) on the
share therefore failed with EINVAL, and a workload in a user namespace
saw its workspace owned by the overflow uid, so chown, tar -x and cp -a
failed as container root.

Clear SB_I_NOIDMAP for virtio-fs once default_permissions is in force.
Nothing changes until a privileged process idmaps a mount explicitly;
the share's own mount keeps its identity mapping.

---
--- a/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
@@ -1583,6 +1583,21 @@
 	err = fuse_fill_super_common(sb, ctx);
 	if (err < 0)
 		goto err_free_fuse_devs;
+
+	/*
+	 * Allow idmapped mounts without FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP from the daemon.
+	 * Upstream requires the daemon to opt in because an idmap-unaware
+	 * daemon checking permissions against the request header uid would
+	 * see the mapped uid. virtio-fs always mounts with
+	 * default_permissions, so the guest kernel makes every permission
+	 * decision through the mount's idmap. Apple's Virtualization.framework
+	 * daemon never negotiates the flag and ignores ownership altogether,
+	 * so without this a user-namespaced workload cannot own its share.
+	 * Only a mount explicitly idmapped with mount_setattr changes; the
+	 * share's own mount is untouched.
+	 */
+	if (fc->default_permissions)
+		sb->s_iflags &= ~SB_I_NOIDMAP;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < fs->nvqs; i++) {
 		struct virtio_fs_vq *fsvq = &fs->vqs[i];
